    "title": "Sound City and Sylvia Massy: Capturing Tool's Debut",
    "content": "Undertow was recorded primarily at Sound City Studios in Van Nuys, with additional work at Grand Master Recorders in Los Angeles. Sound City was already legendary by 1993 — its Neve 8028 console had captured records by Fleetwood Mac, Tom Petty, Nirvana, and Rage Against the Machine. The room itself was prized for its drum sound, the natural acoustics combining with the Neve's character to produce a punchy, dimensional kit recording.\n\n**Producer Sylvia Massy:**\nSylvia Massy came to Undertow with engineering credits on Prince, Tom Petty, and Johnny Cash projects. Her approach to Tool emphasized capturing the band's raw energy rather than polishing it. She was famously willing to bend studio convention — sealing Maynard in a trash can for a vocal take, building unconventional mic configurations to suit the material — but for Danny's kit, her instinct was to trust the source. Pearl Masters into Sound City's room with sympathetic mic placement, minimal processing on the way to tape.\n\n**Microphone Setup (typical of the sessions):**\n- Kick drum: AKG D112 inside, Shure Beta 52A at the port\n- Snare: Shure SM57 top, AKG C451 bottom for wire response\n- Toms: Sennheiser MD421 on each tom — the 90s production standard\n- Hi-hat: AKG C451 angled above the bow\n- Overheads: Neumann KM84 spaced pair for natural stereo image\n- Room mics: Neumann U87 pair capturing Sound City's room character\n\n**The Undertow Drum Sound:**\nWhere many 1993 metal productions favored heavily gated, processed drum sounds, Massy let Danny's kit breathe. Room mics carry significant weight in the mix, giving the drums a sense of space that distinguishes Undertow from the more claustrophobic productions of the era. The natural decay of the Pearl Masters shells is preserved — toms ring musically into the silence between fills rather than being chopped off.\n\n**No Click Track on Most Songs:**\nLike the rest of Tool's catalog, much of Undertow was tracked without a click. Danny's internal tempo and the band's collective feel drove the songs, giving the album a slight elasticity that adds to its organic character. Tempo fluctuations are intentional and emotional rather than corrected.\n\n**The Album in Context:**\nUndertow arrived in April 1993 between Nirvana's \"In Utero\" sessions and the post-grunge wave that would dominate the next two years. Tool didn't fit neatly into any of the surrounding genres — too progressive for grunge, too aggressive for alternative rock, too song-oriented for thrash metal. Massy's production reflected that ambiguity, refusing to push the album toward any single contemporaneous template. The drum sound is part of what makes Undertow still feel like its own thing decades later.",
